{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he sent the young men of the children of Israel.</b> They were the firstborn. By way of the plain sense (peshat) he called them 'young men' because they were pure to offer a sacrifice — they had never tasted the flavor of sin, nor had they approached a woman. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: in the future the young men of Israel who have never tasted sin will give forth a fragrance like Lebanon, as it is said (Hosea 14:7): ״ויהי כזית הודו וריח לו כלבנון״. And with this sacrifice the covenant was completed in the acceptance of the Torah, for Israel entered the covenant through three things: circumcision, immersion, and sacrifice. Circumcision — when Israel came out of Egypt they were circumcised, for all of them had received this commandment by tradition from the fathers. Immersion — as it is written (Exodus 19:10): ״וקדשתם היום ומחר וכבסו שמלותם״. Sacrifice — this is the sacrifice mentioned here. And likewise a person who comes to convert is obligated in all three, as it is said (Numbers 15:15): ״ככם כגר״, and the court is obligated to circumcise him and to immerse him, and when the Holy Temple is rebuilt he shall bring his sacrifice. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded that they first inform him of some commandments and their punishments, and the reason for informing him of the punishments is so that he not convert, for converts are of no benefit to Israel at all — for the mixed multitude were converts, and they were the cause of the calf, and they were also the cause of the plague in the matter of the craving for meat, upon which the thing came to pass, as it is said (Numbers 11:4): ״והאספסוף אשר בקרבו התאוו תאוה״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory stated explicitly: 'Converts are as hard for Israel as a scab,' as it is said (Isaiah 14:1): ״ונספחו על בית יעקב״. And the reason is because they are not expert in the precise details of the commandments, and Israel learn from their deeds." "<b>And there are those</b> who explained that they inform them of some punishments because the reason for this is so that he not say, 'Had I known, I would not have converted,' and his conversion would turn out to be mistaken. Therefore we must inform him of some punishments, so that his conversion be with a whole heart and so that he accept everything upon himself. And what our Rabbis of blessed memory said, 'Converts are hard for Israel' — this is not said to the discredit of the converts but to the discredit of Israel; that is, since the Holy One, blessed be He, sees their intention — that they left their family and the land of their birth and came to cling to the Shekhinah — behold, they hold Israel accountable when they do not serve the Holy One, blessed be He, with a whole heart." "<b>And likewise</b> we find in the Midrash: Resh Lakish said, 'Greater are converts in this time than Israel when they stood at Mount Sinai' — for they saw the voices and the torches and the sound of the shofar and the great and awesome wonders, whereas the converts saw none of this at all, and they come in straits and distress to shelter beneath the wings of the Shekhinah." "<b>And they offered burnt offerings of well-being to YHWH, bulls.</b> All the days of the wilderness Israel feared the attribute of judgment, because they were in a place of destruction and desolation; therefore the sacrifice was bulls. And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:37.295423843Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}